<tr>
<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;"><p align="center" style="margin:0 0 20px 0;"><img alt="An official Justice Department seal is seen at a press conference at the agency's headquarters on April 7, 2026." src="https://www.politico.com/dims4/default/22bf934/2147483647/resize/1000x/quality/90/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fstatic.politico.com%2F2d%2F75%2Fa658b4a2469891a84f876203f29f%2Fhttps-delivery-gettyimages.com%2Fdownloads%2F2269675063" style="width:100%;max-width:500px;" title="US-POLITICS-JUSTICE" width="500"/></p><p style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12px;color:#6E7381;margin-bottom:24px;">The Department of Justice intervened in xAI's lawsuit challenging Colorado's AI law.   |  Kent Nishimura/AFP via Getty Images</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;"><p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">The legal crusade against affirmative action </strong>is coming for artificial intelligence.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">On Friday, the Justice Department intervened in xAI’s challenge to Colorado’s “Consumer Protections for Artificial Intelligence” law. In <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV665J2NPBEylYMJ2aNN8lyfP8EYjQmqkxndB43yzmDqCa4QP9l4RYD7fRdZui5OZVpA/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h2/h001.1p9eOwKQIbmY3cEr8RORx_Q9iAtVyDoj-537JltU3K0" target="_blank"><u>its complaint</u></a>, the DOJ argues the law’s provisions curbing algorithmic bias violates people’s 14th Amendment right to be treated equally under the law.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The intervention is in some ways an outgrowth of the movement to eradicate all race-conscious policies after the landmark Supreme Court case <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV69p5_ghvpY4Sc65VMqWCvfPv1MXqm2taY6e2tsQp76tAQPRSP-WEnEkLOBY3w-grF9iqSmZgmcJXAvJIfBljKik/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h3/h001.ebxUik_yENwcfDNza8X7cnapbK2bmgQud7dnwnk4VJE" target="_blank"><i><u>Students for Fair Admissions v. Harvard</u></i></a> in 2023 struck down affirmative action in college admissions.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Conservative groups like America First Legal, founded by now-White House adviser Stephen Miller, quickly seized on the decision to also <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.dgGKIUEMNqic94f5rSpPKN9cfA5vYXYlUTTdHIQFe4Y1nTbT2U-AJu2zcgpJFwIMbq8N4WVfmDVHs9-_8WXEvNMFXsCpfKjmrcOFFaM7iFAeMy-isAOTwNVhPu0_5N3Y5sR2HUyR8ppW3OvAbGdfTeVt87VXxafxygvXAIoqTmsiZvXPedsbwjIweMssbfrIJKhNbeOtjt4tNwHwdTTmW3PvPIo4EUV3Cjye15jgn5IB7JjW0Euf0InrmwlW1HjcqtjIf3R12F8WJah0bBtnww/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h4/h001.YXsBUNm33B1rRBnQP9TSJxqYoPq5R0oVbIFM02J5dQ8" target="_blank"><u>challenge diversity initiatives</u></a> in other contexts, like hiring and law journal recruiting. The DOJ took up that cause when President Donald Trump returned to office, and even cited the Harvard case in their challenge to the Colorado law.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">This movement is now trying to beat back algorithmic bias laws, significantly narrowing the kinds of discrimination that the government is allowed to combat. Yet it’s unclear how successful these anti-DEI legal arguments will be in the context of machine learning, as opposed to admissions and hiring.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">The Colorado law, in part, obligates developers</strong> to protect consumers from algorithmic discrimination, a phenomenon in which an AI model replicates biases in its training data. A key feature of the law is that it takes aim at disparate impact; a legal concept referring to instances in which an ostensibly neutral policy or system disadvantages a certain group of people — for example, job criteria that leads to disproportionately low minority hires.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The Trump administration has been trying to rid the legal system of the disparate impact concept in the wake of the Harvard case. The president released <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV60SgT1lbNwilsYKFij_SeSSRfSECFHYbB9QTW5S-_OOY59Qp3Dq4lKLlFznVl_biHrn9V5ymxodrerk6Ou_bMeK-kaetWCDaGZCHLnJEijnyMJfNBIlS1S9z70xZKYBxVCqIBiY5Vm-uyIveLU1b40c/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h5/h001.CloWlEmTHkXV5_9Uyx8k2sueb3sNqe7urdfCDtCqQsc" target="_blank"><u>an executive order</u></a> last year to eliminate disparate impact liability, contending the laws force businesses to engage in unconstitutional “racial balancing.” According to Andrew Selbst, a UCLA law professor, the DOJ complaint is aimed at bringing that effort into the realm of AI.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“They're trying to extend [the Harvard ruling] to do what Trump has said that he would do in that executive order and outlaw disparate impact,” said Selbst. “[The Colorado case] is just their vehicle to do it.”</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Disparate impact does not need to be intentional, which is critical when it comes to the issue of algorithmic discrimination. There’s little evidence that developers generally intend for their AI to be biased. In other words, Colorado law says that developers have to address discrimination in algorithms for hiring, housing and other purposes even if the unequal impact is inadvertent.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Yet the DOJ argues in the complaint that such a law violates the 14th Amendment’s equal protection clause by distorting AI model outputs in a way that requires developers and deployers to discriminate based on race, sex, religion and other protected characteristics. It would be hard to meaningfully combat algorithmic discrimination if the government is only allowed to curb purposeful discrimination, according to Chicago-Kent College of Law professor Jason R. Bent, who studies AI and employment.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“The main concern is not that you're intentionally discriminating by setting the parameters in the algorithm based on the training data,” said Bent. “It's that it is going to reproduce, based on the training data, some existing biases or existing disparate outcome.”</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">So does trying to combat algorithmic discrimination </strong>amount to a 14th Amendment violation, similar to the way that it’s illegal to consider race in admissions? The courts haven’t decided on the question, but Bent and Selbst both said that machine learning introduces some unique complications.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Importantly, measures to address algorithmic discrimination mostly involve <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.0EmMx08biZlEUL9L7k4EAPScaZdeLR4vhWWnFormMHPC_xIV7WQmsIRRnkwXngC61uE7_2JCHiUP0PH_o400EA/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h6/h001.4PYaemKm7VqL7u6NPu-O4IdKqf2I_X4EKYHONwsohUQ" target="_blank"><u>adjusting the training data</u></a>. But because AI systems are opaque, it’s unclear whether they’re actually explicitly considering race in their decisionmaking in ways that courts have previously found to be illegal.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“You can have race consciousness in the actual design of the algorithm without ever having race play any part of the model in the actual decision process,” Selbst told DFD.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The Colorado attorney general’s office, which is being sued in xAI’s case, said it would not comment on ongoing litigation. The DOJ and xAI did not respond to inquiries.<br/></p></td>
</tr>
