<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;"><p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">Maine Gov. Janet Mills on Friday vetoed a bill</strong> that would have established the country’s first statewide moratorium on permits for <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV65ZWHEH5gWGSDKLrcKzwr38CGEGQvkfXI1tyYJYGbeuWLjuHnFzFuyQMOFKdeyMuJrLd4hSzR6084KVxhV6NcatWcZehy9JKWZf6sAbn-teeUpI4IeFCf0MFPCODKkpbPQ/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h8/h001.gB99M6s3ulqpzRJ9XUI6gsmGpGUvt5rncrQ0aEi-hJo" target="_blank"><u>data center construction</u></a>.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">In <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV679buCLOudEDVI_QjbwQeazvXMudsOkMqT_1bUd0YZClmKBcNVL2fZ4sUuQ70UXZMJcU2gHmCy3tUILzzVg1b3CF8OI9_iON8alPZuxJdW-HXcpIgdQaxKRF_GAYjQCjj1GDyksAzFYNwFCLvb1y7NgHyNs_Ag7Yd6Yy5PIT4xGM/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h9/h001.8YFfkdhSWZJe-VFGXaa2edkB12wLjrvHhQS9aJtJ-2M" target="_blank"><u>a letter</u></a> to the Maine Legislature, Mills, a Democrat, wrote that she broadly supports a moratorium due to the impacts of data centers on utility prices and the environment in other states, but would not sign the bill because it didn’t include an exemption for a project in Jay, a Maine town that has weathered economic hardship after the closure of a local mill in 2023.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“After prior redevelopment efforts failed, the Town of Jay worked for two years on a $550 million data center redevelopment project to finally bring jobs and investment back to the mill site,” Mills wrote, adding that she would establish a council via executive order to evaluate the impact of data centers in Maine.<br/></p></td>
