<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;"><p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">Elon Musk and Sam Altman are finally going to trial </strong>OpenAI’s for-profit reorganization, Axel Springer’s <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV65ZWHEH5gWGSDKLrcKzwr38gR5U-7DNKZ_ZS7HCZH1LVSzlnm5nIkbaytb9kTwKwGpg7mZrmFSY5ETvfN7nzhvsISYn2_4Ms7rX1Ojt_yfxZHrotIQU6g8Z47hDa9g4CFLvL2TDTrHT04V0wGTfPr_GlK-VWl4yy-XDr47GLystL/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h11/h001.OWLUaEWm3MCP6M8nhG3JJRZv_FLaBmOIBoB7IOa8xJE" target="_blank"><u>Jacob Shamsian reports</u></a>.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Jury selection started Monday to hear a fight over Musk’s claims that he contributed $40 million to OpenAI with the understanding it would stick to its nonprofit mission, but that Altman and other executives bypassed him to establish a for-profit arm. OpenAI argues that Musk knew the company needed to make a profit to be viable, and that his gripes are about not getting more control over the company.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The trial’s witness list has a bevy of heavy hitters, including Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella and OpenAI’s former CTO Mira Murati. Jared Birchall, Musk’s right-hand man, may also testify.<br/></p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
<table width="100%">
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="padding:20px 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#ffffff;background-color:#003034;">
<p style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12px;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:10px;color:#ffffff;">A message from NCTA - The Internet &amp; Television Association:</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:20px;line-height:24px;color:#ffffff;">CBRS is the competition and innovation band because it allows a wide array of users, from businesses to airports and factories to farms, to deploy private networks that don't rely on the Big 3 and that coexist in the same spectrum used by the U.S. military with zero interference.
     <br/>
      
     <br/>
<a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.dgGKIUEMNqic94f5rSpPKCJCpfzLXKLU6KDtDs6qCfbTJD1ervaL6Qj-yGvuh4q9-XxlLDffdIrjWQNw6ENBxdz44rq0ixwj8blv_051E-inr9cnY5NQFDS6ECle7-Mo4VU8s-esjAgdtOX5dV_lmVXOoIbqvnz4aoCbVPYd9KM0J69Ovbp7ULBxTRA953Ei/4q5/cjlR280iQrO3XHmOtjnfNg/h12/h001.Z2B6OChIHVtQSpqnmwhgNw8qKi0SJhmODvcSSKL_h7Y" style="text-decoration:underline;color:#ffffff;" target="_blank">A new study shows that converting fewer than 2% of CBRS base stations to high power would be catastrophic, wasting billions of dollars in private investment.</a> Individual CBRS users would face severe impacts, for instance, John Deere’s connectivity in Illinois would shrink to the range of wireless earbuds. Miami Airport’s network capacity would be cut by one-third, immediately impacting essential activities from passenger security to runway safety.
     <br/>
<br/>
     Thanks to FCC approval in 2020, CBRS is a dynamic spectrum sharing American success story. Now, the FCC must preserve its leadership by protecting CBRS from interference.</p>
